// r24.12 (WO-N3, S22 §C.9): A LEXICAL–ACOUSTIC CONFLICT IS DESCRIBED, NOT
// ASSERTED. emotion2vec read Igor's questioning intonation as `sad` at p ≥ 0.84
// five times in S22 — "Did you miss me since the last session?" (0.986),
// "could you hear me moving around in the room?" (0.977), "I finished the
// thing I was stuck on and it came out well, really well." (1.000), "Say,
// I've always been…" (0.836), "Were you thinking ahead…" (0.968) — and the
// bare tag on his line told the model, in the system prompt's own words, that
// the gap between a sad voice and neutral words "is the most important thing
// in the room". No code defect in the tagger; a SHAPE rule on the tag/text
// pair: a full-strength sad or angry read on words that point the other way
// (his own good news, or net bright evidence) or on a QUESTION carrying no
// heavy word at all is demoted to the tagger's own `, faint` form, so every
// reader applies the scaling WO-88 already applies to a faint tag — the line
// says "[emotion: sad, faint]" (an undertone, per the prompt), the empathy
// check renders "his tone reads sad to me, but only faintly - if it matters,
// asking beats assuming", and emotion_valence_ scales the appraisal input to
// a quarter. The lexical evidence is appraise_text's (with negation and
// tense) and positive_disclosure_text's — not a new list. A faint tag is
// already hedged; a genuinely negative sentence ("I'm sorry, I was sharp with
// you") keeps its full-strength read. The cost, on the record: a genuinely
// angry QUESTION with no heavy word ("Why did you do that?") is hedged too —
// in S22 every full-strength tag on a question was a misread (5 of 5), and
// every real correction carried a heavy word or was not a question.
// ATHENA_TAG_CONFLICT_HEDGE=0 (Config::tag_conflict_hedge, passed by the seam)
// restores the bare tag.
inline bool tag_conflicts_words(const std::string &tag, const std::string &text,
                                const char **why = nullptr) {
    if (why) *why = nullptr;
    if (tag.empty() || tag.find("faint") != std::string::npos) return false;   // already hedged
    if (tag.find("sad") == std::string::npos && tag.find("angry") == std::string::npos) return false;
    const Appraisal ap = appraise_text(text, /*v2=*/true);
    const float pos = ap.pos_w + (aff2::positive_disclosure_text(text) ? 1.0f : 0.0f);
    if (pos > ap.neg_w) { if (why) *why = "his words point the other way"; return true; }
    if (ap.neg_w == 0.0f && interrogative_shape(text)) {
        // r24.13 (WO-W9): …unless the read is ANGRY. See the paragraph on
        // tag_hedge_angry_q_on() above: the five S22 misreads this arm exists
        // for were all `sad`; anger in a bare question is carried by prosody
        // alone, which is the tagger's whole competence and the lexicon's
        // blind spot, so demoting it discards the only evidence there is.
        // ATHENA_TAG_HEDGE_ANGRY_Q=0 restores r24.12 (both tags demoted).
        if (tag_hedge_angry_q_on() && tag.find("angry") != std::string::npos)
            return false;
        if (why) *why = "a question carrying nothing heavy";
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
// The tag the turn carries: the tagger's read, or its `, faint` form on a
// conflict. `on` is Config::tag_conflict_hedge (the seam reads it from the
// Mind); off, the tag passes through untouched — r24.11 byte for byte.
inline std::string hedged_tag(const std::string &tag, const std::string &text, bool on,
                              const char **why = nullptr) {
    if (why) *why = nullptr;
    if (!on || !tag_conflicts_words(tag, text, why)) return tag;
    const size_t rb = tag.rfind(']');
    if (rb == std::string::npos) return tag;
    return tag.substr(0, rb) + ", faint" + tag.substr(rb);
}
